    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sumerian, the language of the city-states of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, is with Egyptian one of the two earliest written languages: its speakers developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> writing, at first to keep accounts, around 3200 BCE.</li><li>A language isolate unrelated to any other, it gave way as a spoken language to Akkadian around 2000 BCE, but remained the classical language of Mesopotamian religion and learning for nearly two thousand years.</li><li>Its literature, from the hymns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a> to the first stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, its law codes and its scribal schools make it one of the foundations of written civilisation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Sumerians counted in sixties, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> of their Babylonian heirs their system survives in the sixty minutes of an hour and the 360 degrees of a circle.</p></aside>
    </section>

