    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 3200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first accounting tablets are written at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> in proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The earliest Sumerian literary texts are copied at Shuruppak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a>, high priestess at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, composes hymns in Sumerian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The law code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, the oldest known, is written in Sumerian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Babylonian scribal schools copy the classics of Sumerian literature, long after the language has ceased to be spoken</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 80</span><span class="rf-text">The last datable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablet, an astronomical almanac, is written at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1869</span><span class="rf-text">The scholar Jules Oppert gives the rediscovered language the name Sumerian</span></li></ol>
    </section>

