    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur.jpg" alt="Ur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urimki_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steve Harris, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sumerian was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, the land of the great cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, Eridu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, between the lower courses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its speakers called it eme-gi, perhaps “the native tongue”, and their land Kiengi; the name Sumer comes from Akkadian. Where the Sumerians came from, and when they arrived in Mesopotamia, is unknown. Their language is an isolate: it has no known relatives, living or dead, despite many attempts to connect it with languages from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p>The Sumerians played the leading part in one of the great inventions of history, writing. In the late 4th millennium BCE the temples and administrators of Uruk, the largest city of its time, needed to record goods, rations, animals and labour. Clay tokens and numerical tablets were followed, around 3200 BCE, by the first tablets combining numbers with signs for things and people, called proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. Most of these earliest texts are accounts; others are lists of words, used to train scribes.</p><p>At first the signs stood for words and things, not sounds, and it is difficult to tell which language the earliest tablets record. The use of signs for their sound value, as in a rebus, shows that the language was Sumerian: the sign for “arrow”, ti in Sumerian, was used to write the Sumerian word ti, “life”. Over the following centuries the system was extended to write names, grammar and eventually any sentence.</p><p>By the middle of the 3rd millennium BCE, Sumerian was written in royal inscriptions, contracts, letters and the first works of literature, and it was the dominant language of public life in the south of Mesopotamia, while Akkadian, a Semitic language, was spoken in the north and increasingly in the south as well.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list.jpg" alt="Sumerian King List" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weld-Blundell_Prism_with_transcription_by_Stephen_Herbert_Langdon_(1876-1937).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph: unknown&amp;gt; Transcription: Stephen Herbert Langdon (1876-1937), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sumerian was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cuneus, “wedge”: signs made of wedge-shaped strokes pressed into soft clay with a cut reed. The earliest signs were drawings of objects, written in columns, but over time they were turned on their side and simplified into abstract combinations of wedges, written from left to right. Several hundred signs were in use at any period. A sign could stand for a whole word, for a syllable or, as a silent classifier, for the category of the word that followed it, such as a god, a place or an object of wood.</p><p>The grammar of Sumerian is very different from that of the Semitic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>. It is agglutinative: words are built from a root followed or preceded by chains of separate elements, each with one meaning, so that a single verbal form can express the subject, the object, direction, location and mood. It is also ergative: the subject of a transitive verb is marked differently from the subject of an intransitive one. The usual word order was subject, object and verb. Nouns were divided into two classes, human and non-human.</p><p>Because Sumerian had many words that sounded alike, scholars have suggested that it may have used tones, as Chinese does, but this cannot be proved. Much of its pronunciation is known only through the way later Akkadian scribes transcribed it.</p><p>Besides the main dialect, Emegir, Sumerian texts use a form called Emesal, often translated “fine tongue”, used for the speech of goddesses and women in literature and for the laments sung by a class of priests. Sumerian vocabulary includes words such as lugal, “king”, literally “big man”, dingir, “god”, and e-gal, “big house” or palace, which passed through Akkadian into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic as words for a palace or temple.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nippur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nippur.jpg" alt="Nippur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jasmine N. Walthall, U.S. Army, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 3rd millennium BCE Sumerian was spoken in the independent city-states of the south, which were ruled by kings and governors and competed for land and water. Akkadian speakers lived among them, especially in the north, and bilingualism was common. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around 2300 BCE, Akkadian became a language of government alongside Sumerian.</p><p>Sumerian enjoyed a last flourishing in the late 3rd millennium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> around 2100 BCE, left long Sumerian inscriptions on statues and clay cylinders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, which ruled Mesopotamia from about 2112 to 2004 BCE, used Sumerian for a vast administration that has left tens of thousands of tablets, as well as for royal hymns praising its kings.</p><p>As a spoken language, Sumerian gave way to Akkadian, probably between about 2100 and 1800 BCE; scholars debate exactly when it ceased to be anyone’s mother tongue. It did not die as a written language. In the scribal schools of the Old Babylonian period, the “tablet houses”, Akkadian-speaking pupils learned Sumerian as a classical language, copying its literature, word lists and proverbs; much of what is known of Sumerian literature comes from tablets written by such students at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> in the 18th century BCE.</p><p>For the next two thousand years Sumerian remained the language of religious ritual, incantation and learning, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Scribes produced bilingual texts with Akkadian translations between the lines, and the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, in the 7th century BCE, boasted that he could read the difficult texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a>, the script invented for Sumerian, was adapted to write Akkadian, Elamite, Hittite, Hurrian and other languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enheduanna.jpg" alt="Enheduanna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enheduanna,_daughter_of_Sargon_of_Akkad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mefman00, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sumerian has the oldest literature in the world. The earliest literary texts, from Shuruppak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>, around 2600 BCE, include proverbs of wisdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak">Instructions of Shuruppak</a>, and hymns such as the Kesh Temple Hymn. Around 2300 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a>, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> and high priestess of the moon god at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, composed hymns to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> and to the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>; she is often described as the first author in history known by name.</p><p>The richest body of literature was copied in the schools of the early 2nd millennium BCE: epics of the heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmerkar">Enmerkar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugalbanda">Lugalbanda</a> and Bilgamesh, better known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, whose adventures in five Sumerian poems were later drawn on for the Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>; myths such as the Descent of Inanna to the underworld; a Sumerian story of the flood, with its hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Ziusudra</a>; laments over the destruction of cities, such as the Lament for Ur; debate poems, such as that between Summer and Winter; love songs, proverbs and letters.</p><p>Sumerian was also the language of the first law codes. The code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, around 2100 BCE, is the oldest known, three centuries older than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, and the code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lipit-ishtar">Lipit-Ishtar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> followed in the 20th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> recorded the dynasties believed to have ruled since kingship “descended from heaven”.</p><p>The Sumerian scribal tradition also laid the foundations of mathematics, with its system of counting in sixties and its tables, of lexicography, with its lists of words grouped by subject, and of education: school texts describe the hard life of pupils, their teachers and their beatings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gudea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="Gudea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jastrow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Over its long history, Sumerian changed as any language does. Scholars distinguish the archaic language of the earliest texts, Old Sumerian of the mid-3rd millennium, the Neo-Sumerian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, and the literary Sumerian of the Old Babylonian schools, written by people for whom it was no longer a mother tongue and increasingly influenced by Akkadian.</p><p>As an isolate, Sumerian left no descendants. Its influence lived on in Akkadian, which borrowed many of its words and may owe to it its unusual word order, with the verb at the end, and through Akkadian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Arabic.</p><p>Sumerian continued to be copied in temples into the last centuries BCE, in bilingual hymns, incantations and laments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> writing itself came to an end in the 1st century CE: the last datable cuneiform tablet, an astronomical almanac from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, was written for the year 79 to 80 CE. Knowledge of the language and its script was then lost for nearly two thousand years.</p><p>It was recovered in the 19th century, when scholars deciphering Akkadian cuneiform noticed texts in another, non-Semitic language. The Irish scholar Edward Hincks suggested in 1850 that cuneiform had been invented by speakers of a language other than Akkadian, and in 1869 Jules Oppert named it Sumerian. Some scholars long denied that it had ever been a real language, but excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> from 1877 revealed the monuments of the Sumerians, and the grammar of their language was worked out in the 20th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziusudra.jpg" alt="Ziusudra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Ziusudra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumerian_King_List,_1800_BC,_Larsa,_Iraq_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gts-tg, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sumerian stands at the beginning of written history. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> script developed for it was used for some three thousand years and for more than a dozen languages, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and it gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> its first archives, libraries and schools.</p><p>Its literature preserved the earliest versions of stories that travelled far: the adventures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> and a great flood, whose survivor parallels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Utnapishtim</a> of the Akkadian epic and Noah of the Bible. Its law codes, hymns, proverbs and laments are the oldest of their kinds.</p><p>The Sumerian habit of counting in sixties, developed by Babylonian astronomers, survives in the sixty minutes of an hour, the sixty seconds of a minute and the 360 degrees of a circle. A few Sumerian words survive, through Akkadian and other languages, in the vocabulary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><p>Today Sumerian is studied in universities around the world, and dictionaries and digital corpora have made its texts more accessible than at any time since the scribal schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. More than a hundred thousand Sumerian texts have been recovered from the soil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and many still await publication.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sumerian, the language of the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and an isolate with no known relatives, was written from around 3200 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, which its speakers developed. Spoken until around 2000 BCE, it remained the classical language of Mesopotamian religion and scholarship until the 1st century CE, and its literature, laws and counting in sixties are among the foundations of written civilisation.</em></p>

